  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.",
  "text": "The elevator stopped between the ninth and tenth floors. A low, grinding hum vibrated through the metal walls, a sound that felt less like machinery and more like a dying animal. Elias pressed the call button again, his knuckles white against the brushed steel panel. The digital display above the door flickered, the number '9' turning into a jagged, unrecognizable symbol before vanishing entirely. Silence rushed in to fill the void, heavy and suffocating. He checked his watch; the second hand had stopped moving. It wasn't just the elevator that was broken; it was time itself, or at least his perception of it. The lights flickered once, casting long, distorted shadows across the small, confined space. He could hear his own breathing, loud and ragged, echoing off the mirrored walls. A drop of condensation fell from the ceiling, landing on his shoulder with a cold shock that made him flinch. He wasn't alone. The thought crept in, subtle but undeniable. The air smelled of ozone and old dust, a scent that hadn't been present when he had stepped in three minutes ago. He turned slowly, his eyes scanning the dark corners of the car. There was no one there, yet the feeling of being watched intensified, pressing against the back of his neck. The grinding noise returned, louder this time, accompanied by a sharp jolt that threw him against the wall. The doors shuddered, then began to open, revealing not the hallway of the tenth floor, but a pitch-black void that seemed to stretch on forever. A single, pale hand reached out from the darkness, fingers long and trembling, beckoning him to step forward."
}

